  "activity": {
    "id": "act_retrograde_motion",
    "name": "Understanding Apparent Retrograde Motion of Mars",
    "description": "A seven-step AR investigation of apparent retrograde motion. Learners first observe Mars looping across the night sky, examine the geocentric epicycle explanation, adopt the heliocentric outside view, link the two perspectives via line-of-sight, physically walk Earth's orbit to embody the effect, compare Mars/Jupiter/Saturn loops, and finish with a summary recap. Aligned to objectives on describing the loop, explaining it via Earth overtaking a slower planet, contrasting geocentric vs heliocentric parsimony, and comparing loop sizes across outer planets.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "wp_retrograde_motion",
    "start": "step_observe_sky",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_observe_sky",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "dev_hololens",
        "location": "place_observation_deck",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. Observe Mars in the Night Sky",
          "description": "Stand on virtual Earth at night and look up at the ecliptic constellations Leo, Virgo, Libra, and Scorpio. Watch the red point of Mars over six simulated months: it first drifts slowly eastward, then halts and loops westward for about two months (roughly 15 degrees), then reverses and resumes eastward. A trail marks its full path. Why would a planet move backward? Say or click 'next' to investigate."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_starfield",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_intro"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_mars_trail_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_mars",
              "poi": "label_anchor",
              "text": "Mars"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_mars",
              "type": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_starfield",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_geocentric",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          },
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "step_geocentric",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_geocentric",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "dev_hololens",
        "location": "place_observation_deck",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. The Geocentric Explanation (Epicycles)",
          "description": "With Earth at the center, Mars rides a small circle (the epicycle) that itself rides a larger circle (the deferent). Watch the machinery run: when Mars reaches the inner part of its epicycle, closest to Earth, its combined motion appears to reverse. This reproduces the loop, but only with carefully tuned circle sizes and speeds, and more epicycles must be stacked to keep predictions accurate. Click or say 'next' for a simpler answer."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_epicycle_rig",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_geocentric"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_epicycle_rig",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_epicycle_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_epicycle_rig",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_epicycle",
              "poi": "epicycle",
              "text": "Epicycle"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_epicycle_rig",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_label_deferent",
              "poi": "deferent",
              "text": "Deferent"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_epicycle_rig",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_heliocentric",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          },
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "step_heliocentric",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_heliocentric",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "dev_hololens",
        "location": "place_solar_platform",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. The Heliocentric View",
          "description": "Rise above the ecliptic plane to a vantage point outside the solar system. The Sun sits at the center; Earth orbits at 1 AU (period 1 year) and Mars at 1.52 AU (period 1.88 years). Watch the planets move at correct relative speeds and see Earth catch up to and overtake Mars about every 26 months. The retrograde event replays here with no epicycles at all, just two smooth ellipses. Click or say 'next' to link the views."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_solar_system",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_narration_heliocentric"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_solar_system",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_orbits_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_solar_system",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_linkage",
